::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: The station clock had stopped, but the benches were polished by years of travelers. Source note: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize movement, memory, and connection. Which research question best fits? {
=How does the stopped station clock and worn seating develop the idea of memory in a public space?#Correct. This question can be answered with the passage and source note.
~Who built the station clock and benches?#This asks for historical facts not provided by the literary passage.
~What kind of wood are the benches made from?#This detail does not support the Unit 4 research focus.
~Why are train stations better than markets?#This adds an unsupported comparison.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source note: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize movement, memory, and connection. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~Transit spaces are always happy places in literature.#This adds an unsupported emotion.
~Literature should include trains when discussing memory.#This changes the source idea.
=Places connected to travel can represent movement, remembered experiences, and human connection.#Correct. This accurately restates the source note.
~Transit spaces are only symbols when a clock appears.#This adds a condition not in the source.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: The station clock had stopped, but the benches were polished by years of travelers. Which literary analysis claim is strongest? {
~The station has a clock and benches.#This is summary only.
=The stopped clock and worn benches create a contrast between time frozen and memory preserved through use.#Correct. This is a defensible literary analysis claim.
~The station should repair its clock.#This is practical advice.
~Travelers probably liked sitting on the benches.#This is unsupported speculation.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An essay begins with an empty station, gives examples of travelers who passed through it, and ends by arguing that public places preserve memory. Which organization claim is clearest? {
~The essay has a beginning, middle, and end.#This is too general.
~The essay is about a station and travelers.#This summarizes subject only.
~The essay should start with the final claim.#This is preference, not analysis.
=The essay moves from image to examples to claim, helping readers understand how a public place can preserve memory.#Correct. This analyzes organization and meaning.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The station thing shows memory and the essay is organized good. Which revision uses academic voice? {
~The station is really good at showing memory, and the essay is good too.#This remains informal and vague.
~The station thing is about memory because the essay talks about it.#This is vague.
=The station image develops the idea of public memory, and the essay's image-to-claim organization clarifies that meaning.#Correct. This uses academic voice and precise wording.
~The station memory is nice and organized.#This is unclear and informal.
}
